{"type": "message", "id": "46226aaa", "parentId": "1b6bf811", "timestamp": "2026-04-08T11:20:44.635Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Excellent! Now I have the exact quarterly revenue figures from the SEC filing (Tesla's First Quarter 2024 Update, which includes quarterly comparisons):\n\nFrom the SEC filing, the quarterly revenues are:\n- Q1-2023: $23,329 million\n- Q2-2023: $24,927 million\n- Q3-2023: $23,350 million\n- Q4-2023: $25,167 million\n\nAnd from the same SEC filing or related ones, the GAAP net income figures:\n- Q1-2023: $2,513 million\n- Q2-2023: $2,703 million\n- Q3-2023: $1,853 million\n\n\n- Q4-2023: $7,928 million\n\nNow I'm calculating the net profit margin for each quarter using the formula (Net Income / Total Revenues) × 100. Q1 comes to about 10.78%, Q2 to about 10.84%, Q3 to about 7.94%, and Q4 to about 31.50%. So only Q1 and Q2 crossed the 10.5% threshold. I need to verify the Q3-2023 revenue figure more carefully from the SEC filing data I found earlier. I've confirmed the quarterly revenue figures from the SEC filing, so now I'm calculating the net profit margins for each quarter. Q1 comes in at 10.78%, Q2 at 10.84%, and Q3 at 7.94% — all using the straightforward formula of net income divided by total revenues. The answer is Q1 and Q2.", "thinkingSignature": "reasoning"}, {"type": "text", "text": "I now have all the data needed. Let me compile the figures from the SEC filings:\n\n**From Tesla's SEC filings (10-Qs and 10-K):**\n\n| Quarter | Net Income (GAAP) | Total Revenues | Net Profit Margin |\n|---------|-------------------|----------------|-------------------|\n| Q1 (Mar 31, 2023) | $2,513M | $23,329M | 10.77% |\n| Q2 (Jun 30, 2023) | $2,703M | $24,927M | 10.84% |\n| Q3 (Sep 30, 2023) | $1,853M | $23,350M | 7.94% |\n| Q4 (Dec 31, 2023) | $7,928M | $25,167M | 31.50% |\n\n**Net profit margin > 10.5%?**  \n- Q1: 10.77% ✓  \n- Q2: 10.84% ✓  \n- Q3: 7.94% ✗  \n- Q4: 31.50% ✓ (but this was in